<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=diesel-engine">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diesel engine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as compression-ignition engine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A diesel engine is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/internal-combustion-engine">internal combustion engine</a> in which the fuel is ignited by the heat of air compressed in the cylinder, without a spark plug. The German engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-diesel">Rudolf Diesel</a> published his idea in 1893 in Theory and Construction of a Rational Heat Motor, and his first prototype was built that summer at the Maschinenfabrik <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, with the first ignition on 10 August 1893. A redesigned engine ran for 88 revolutions in February 1894, and the third prototype, tested on 17 February 1897, reached an effective efficiency of 26.2%, against the 6–10% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engines</a> that Diesel had learned of as a student.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diesel-engine.jpg" alt="Diesel engine" width="330" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: High Contrast · CC BY 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MAN_TGX_V8_engine.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1893</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Augsburg</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-diesel">Rudolf Diesel</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Critics, among them Otto Köhler and Emil Capitaine, claimed that Diesel had invented nothing new; Capitaine lost a patent lawsuit against him. Diesel may have taken the idea of air-blast injection from George B. Brayton.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/internal-combustion-engine">Internal combustion engine<span>1794</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=diesel-engine">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
